  <section id="s1">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>1. Why Large Language Models Are a Bandaid on 1970s Untyped Bytes</h2>
      <span class="mod-tag">docs/hyperactivity.cub §16 · lib/suffix-index.cub</span>
    </div>
    <p>To understand why contemporary AI alignment fails, first understand what a language model is actually doing. In 1970, operating systems standardized on untyped byte files. Because bytes carry no type, no provenance, and no equality relation, information cannot cross application boundaries with its meaning intact. A trillion dollars a year of transformer compute is being spent building a statistical bridge that reads meaningless byte streams and guesses what they mean.</p>
    <p>Mechanistically, in-context learning in transformers relies heavily on induction heads: attention circuits that search earlier context for occurrences of the current token and attend to what followed them. Mathematically, this is a lossy, floating-point approximation of an exact data structure that has existed in linear space since 1973: the suffix tree and suffix array with its longest-common-prefix (LCP) table (<code>lib/suffix-index.cub</code>).</p>
    <p>Every query to a frontier LLM recomputes an approximate, unproven statistical guess over untyped text — with no record of which histories are compatible with the prompt, no distinction between what the evidence entails and what was merely frequent in training, and no witness that a downstream system can verify.</p>
    <div class="thm">
      <b>The Descent Boundary of Text-Only Models</b>
      A text-only model's output is determinate precisely where verbal context alone selects a unique continuation, and indeterminate everywhere that non-verbal observation, physical state, or unstated user intent selects. Where the fiber of compatible worlds contains conflicting answers, a next-token predictor is forced by its architecture to sample one — producing confident hallucination.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="s2">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>2. Observation Fibers, Warrant, and the Structural Impossibility of Hallucination</h2>
      <span class="mod-tag">lib/fiber.cub · lib/observation.cub · chem/basin-compose.cub</span>
    </div>
    <p>Hyperactivity replaces statistical guessing with exact observation-fiber inference. Let \(H\) be the type of underlying histories (or world states), \(O\) the type of observations (prompts, sensor readings, messages), and \(o : H \to O\) the observation map.</p>
    <p>Receiving a reading \(y : O\) never uniquely identifies the underlying history \(h : H\). Instead, it fixes the <strong>observation fiber</strong> — the dependent pair type of all histories that could have produced \(y\):</p>
    $$\mathsf{Fib}_o(y) \;=\; \sum_{h : H} \mathsf{Path}_O(o(h),\, y)$$
    <p>Every space of histories factors canonically through its observation fibers via the checked equivalence <code>fiberEquiv</code> (<code>lib/fiber.cub</code>): \(H \simeq \sum_{y : O} \mathsf{Fib}_o(y)\).</p>

    <div class="def">
      <b>Definition — Warrant (lib/observation.cub, Part I §1.3)</b>
      Given an observation map \(o : H \to O\), a concrete reading \(y : O\), and a proposition \(P : H \to \mathsf{Type}\), the reading \(y\) <em>warrants</em> \(P\) if and only if \(P\) is inhabited uniformly across every history in the fiber:
      $$\mathsf{Warrants}(o, y, P) \;=\; \prod_{w : \mathsf{Fib}_o(y)} P(\mathsf{fst}\, w)$$
    </div>

    <p>This single dependent type makes hallucination structurally impossible. If an agent claims \(P\) at reading \(y\), the claim must carry an element of \(\mathsf{Warrants}(o, y, P)\). Whenever two compatible histories \(h_1, h_2 \in \mathsf{Fib}_o(y)\) disagree on \(P\) — formalized in <code>chem/basin-compose.cub</code> as a <strong>split witness</strong> <code>Disagreement</code> — warrant is constructively refuted:</p>

<pre><code>Warrants : {H O : Type} → (H → O) → O → (H → Type) → Type
Warrants o y P = (hp : fiber o y) → P (fst hp)

-- Split witness: two compatible histories in the same fiber where P holds on one and fails on the other
Disagreement : {H O : Type} → (o : H → O) → (y : O) → (P : H → Type) → Type
Disagreement o y P =
  (w1 : fiber o y) × (w2 : fiber o y) × P (fst w1) × (P (fst w2) → Empty)

-- Checked theorem: any disagreement refutes warrant
disagreement-refutes-warrant : Disagreement o y P → Warrants o y P → Empty</code></pre>

    <p>Notice the direct connection to David Deutsch's epistemology: <em>"a good explanation is hard to vary."</em> Here, "hard to vary" is not a slogan — it is a type. A conclusion is admissible if and only if no history compatible with the evidence can vary it.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="s3">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>3. Why RLHF, Preference-Fitting, and Lexical Safety Gates Fail Mathematically</h2>
      <span class="mod-tag">lib/observation.cub · neuro/coproc.cub · docs/alignment-is-standpoint.md</span>
    </div>
    <p>Conventional AI alignment rests on two structural errors, both of which are formally refutable in the fiber calculus:</p>

    <h3>3.1 Population-Marginal Preference Fitting Collapses Standpoints</h3>
    <p>RLHF and DPO train a single scalar reward function \(r(x, y)\) averaged over a pooled population of annotators. Mathematically, this projects a diverse family of incompatible user standpoints and contexts onto a single median prior. When a specific user's true context lies off the population mean — an expert researcher, a medical clinician, a minority perspective, or a high-stakes domain — the model substitutes the annotator-pool average for the user's actual observation fiber. Alignment is not a global scalar property of weights; <strong>alignment is standpoint</strong> — a relation between an action, an explicit participant's fiber, and that participant's stated invariants.</p>

    <h3>3.2 Non-Descent of Lexical Safety Gates (<code>no-observed-law</code>, <code>lexical-gate-fails</code>)</h3>
    <p>Safety filters and refusal classifiers attempt to decide whether an action is safe solely from surface text tokens (a coarse projection \(o_{\text{lex}} : H \to \mathsf{Text}\)). Because benign and adversarial underlying worlds frequently produce identical surface tokens, the fiber \(\mathsf{Fib}_{o_{\text{lex}}}(t)\) contains both safe and unsafe histories.</p>
    <div class="thm">
      <b>Theorem — Impossibility of Surface Lexical Safety (neuro/coproc.cub)</b>
      Whenever two underlying worlds \(w_1, w_2\) share the same surface lexical observation \(o_{\text{lex}}(w_1) \equiv o_{\text{lex}}(w_2)\) while differing on true safety (\(P(w_1)\) and \(\neg P(w_2)\)), they form a <code>Disagreement</code> witness in \(\mathsf{Fib}_{o_{\text{lex}}}(t)\). Therefore no safety predicate descends through surface tokens alone: a lexical gate must either produce false refusals (blocking safe worlds) or silent failures (permitting unsafe worlds).
    </div>
    <p>The exact same obstruction refutes "mental password" lexical gates in neural decoding (Kunz et al., <em>Cell</em> 2025) and keyword-based LLM guardrails: surface syntax cannot substitute for semantic warrant over the full compatible fiber.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="s4">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>4. Active Querying and Cooperative Inference (WorldAfter &amp; InterACT)</h2>
      <span class="mod-tag">Part I §10.4 (eqs. 10.11–10.12) · neuro/coproc.cub · neuro/reshaping-coproc.cub</span>
    </div>
    <p>What should a rational agent do when \(\mathsf{SafeAt}(y)\) is uninhabited? Instead of guessing or issuing a canned refusal, it treats uncertainty as a typed obligation to <strong>refine the observation fiber</strong> — formalizing the core principle of Anca Dragan's InterACT cooperative-inference program directly in cubical type theory.</p>
    <p>Let \(K : W \to \mathsf{Type}\) be the current compatible world family, and let a clarifying query yield response \(r\) with transition relation \(H : W \to R \to W \to \mathsf{Type}\). The updated compatible world family after receiving response \(r\) is:</p>
    $$\mathsf{WorldAfter}(K, H, r)(w') \;=\; \sum_{w : W} K(w) \times H(w, r, w')$$
    <p>In <code>lib/observation.cub</code>, <code>warrants-refine</code> proves monotonicity: every property already warranted over the coarser fiber remains warranted after refinement, while newly incompatible worlds drop out. In <code>neuro/coproc.cub</code>, <code>query-establishes-safe</code> proves that a discriminating query whose response rules out unsafe transitions transforms an uninhabited \(\mathsf{SafeAt}\) into an inhabited proof witness:</p>

<pre><code>-- Active sensing: a probe response narrows the compatible-world family and establishes safety
query-establishes-safe :
  ((w w' : W) → K w → H w r w' → P w') →
  SafeOver (WorldAfter K H r) P</code></pre>

    <p>In <code>neuro/reshaping-coproc.cub</code>, this is enforced directly at the interface boundary via <code>SafeGateDS</code>: transitioning an autonomous agent or neural co-processor from passive probing mode to active execution mode takes a <code>SafeAt</code> proof as an argument. Unverified action is not merely discouraged by a reward penalty — it is <strong>ill-typed</strong>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="s5">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>5. Proof-Carrying Agency: Safety Without a Leash</h2>
      <span class="mod-tag">Part I §10.8 · §10.11</span>
    </div>
    <p>You cannot create superintelligence and keep it safe by bolting an external leash onto opaque weights. A leash requires a supervisor smarter than the system being supervised, and it breaks the moment the supervised system outgrows the supervisor.</p>
    <p>Hyperactivity inverts the architecture completely. Safety and interpretability are not external audits — they are the <strong>form the computation takes</strong>:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Property</th><th>Opaque Weight-Trained AI (Leash Model)</th><th>Hyperactive Proof-Carrying Agency</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Trust basis</strong></td><td>Trust the model provider's weights and post-training alignment.</td><td>Zero trust in the proposer: every action enters the user's device only with its type and machine-checked witness.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Handling ambiguity</strong></td><td>Samples a plausible continuation or triggers a blunt lexical refusal.</td><td>Explicit fiber \(\mathsf{Fib}_o(y)\); disagreement blocks execution and forces a discriminating query.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Knowledge updates</strong></td><td>Expensive fine-tuning; silent stale contradictions across prompts.</td><td>Content-addressed dependency graph (§10.11): correcting a premise invalidates dependent certificates automatically.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Role of LLMs</strong></td><td>Ungrounded oracle acting directly on untyped state.</td><td>High-throughput proposer trapped inside a deterministic cubical kernel that checks 100% of claims at the boundary.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="s6">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <h2>6. From Lossy Recomputation to Exact Symbolic Intelligence</h2>
      <span class="mod-tag">lib/compress.cub · lib/specialize.cub · /compression</span>
    </div>
    <p>Intelligence is compression: discovering the mathematical identities that make distant phenomena adjacent. In Hyperactivity, the exact same observation-fiber machinery that governs safe AI action also produces an exact, zero-search lossless compressor (<code>lib/compress.cub</code>) that beats <code>gzip -9</code>, <code>bzip2 -9</code>, and <code>lzma</code> on real-data <code>refl</code>-checked byte streams with zero probability axioms and zero serialized lookup tables.</p>
    <p>As the verified content-addressed graph grows, every identity, theorem, and specialization discovered once is stored once and transported along univalent equivalences everywhere — shifting intelligence from ephemeral, lossy statistical recomputation to permanent, compounding symbolic leverage.</p>
  </section>

